// io_read_n_bytes — SELF-HOSTED `io.read_n_bytes(n)` over the WASI stdin-N-bytes floor
// (prim.read_n_bytes), the v1 trust-spine realization of `io.read_n_bytes`. Reads UP TO `n` bytes
// of standard input (fd 0) and returns them as a `List[Int]` (each byte 0..255 as one element);
// fewer than `n` at EOF. The SIBLING of io_read_line (which builds a String); this builds a
// List[Int]. The prim packages the chunked fd_read loop and the list construction; the result is a
// fresh OWNED List[Int] (a flat block — its i64 elements own no nested handles, so the scope-end
// Drop frees just the block: no leak). Because this body reaches prim.read_n_bytes, its cap_witness
// carries Capability::Stdin — the SAME distinct capability io.read_line carries — so a caller is
// caps-verified ONLY if it declares Stdin (an `effect fn`). NON-DETERMINISTIC (reads live stdin):
// no byte-match unless stdin is fixed. Matches io.almd's `fn read_n_bytes(n: Int) -> List[Int]`.
//
// THE PER-CALL MAXIMUM is 2^26 bytes (64 MiB), and it is a real part of the contract
// rather than an implementation detail, because `n` is a LIMIT and the answer is already
// allowed to be shorter ("fewer than n at EOF" — the same latitude POSIX read() takes).
//
// It exists because `prim.read_n_bytes` takes an i32 at the host floor, so a count that
// does not fit arrived TRUNCATED: `read_n_bytes(i64::MAX)` became -1 and this leg returned
// NOTHING where native read every available byte, and 2^32 became 0. Clamping in the
// semantics layer means the floor only ever sees a count it can represent, and 64 MiB is
// MEASURED, not chosen: the wasm buffer allocates at 2^26 and fails at 2^27. Native applies
// the identical clamp (ALMIDE_IO_READ_MAX_BYTES, runtime/rs/src/io.rs), so a caller that
// wants more loops until the answer is empty — on either target, identically.
fn io_read_n_bytes(n: Int) -> List[Int] = if n <= 0 then prim.read_n_bytes(0)
else if n > 67108864 then prim.read_n_bytes(67108864)
else prim.read_n_bytes(n)
